Create a configuration class that enables caching and defines per-cache TTLs:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableCaching // Activates proxy-based cache interception
public class RedisCacheConfig {

    @Bean
    public RedisCacheManager cacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory cf) {
        // Default config: 10-min TTL, JSON serialization
        RedisCacheConfiguration defaults = RedisCacheConfiguration
            .defaultCacheConfig()
            .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(10))
            .serializeValuesWith(
                SerializationPair.fromSerializer(
                    new GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer()));

        // Per-cache overrides
        Map<String, RedisCacheConfiguration> perCache = Map.of(
            "users",    defaults.entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(30)),
            "products", defaults.entryTtl(Duration.ofHours(1)),
            "pricing",  defaults.entryTtl(Duration.ofSeconds(60))
        );

        return RedisCacheManager.builder(cf)
            .cacheDefaults(defaults)
            .withInitialCacheConfigurations(perCache)
            .build();
    }
}
```

**Why GenericJackson2JsonRedisSerializer?** It stores human-readable JSON in Redis, making debugging with `redis-cli` easy. JDK serialization is opaque and breaks across class version changes.

Apply cache annotations to your service methods — no caching logic leaks into business code:

```java
@Service
public class UserService {

    @Cacheable(value = "users", key = "#id")
    public User findById(Long id) {
        // Only called on cache miss — result is cached automatically
        return userRepository.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(id));
    }

    @CachePut(value = "users", key = "#user.id")
    public User updateUser(User user) {
        // Always executes and updates the cache with the return value
        return userRepository.save(user);
    }

    @CacheEvict(value = "users", key = "#id")
    public void deleteUser(Long id) {
        // Removes the cache entry after the method executes
        userRepository.deleteById(id);
    }

    @CacheEvict(value = "users", allEntries = true)
    public void refreshAllUsers() {
        // Clears the entire "users" cache namespace
    }
}
```

The proxy intercepts calls at the bean boundary. Internal calls within the same class bypass the cache — this is the most common source of "why isn't my cache working?" bugs.

Use SpEL expressions for composite keys, conditional caching, and selective eviction:

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {

    // Composite key from multiple arguments
    @Cacheable(value = "products",
               key = "#category + ':' + #page + ':' + #size")
    public Page<Product> findByCategory(String category, int page, int size) {
        return productRepository.findByCategory(category, PageRequest.of(page, size));
    }

    // Only cache if result has items (don't cache empty results)
    @Cacheable(value = "search",
               key = "#query",
               unless = "#result.isEmpty()")
    public List<Product> search(String query) {
        return searchService.search(query);
    }

    // Skip cache for admin users who always need fresh data
    @Cacheable(value = "products",
               key = "#id",
               condition = "#requestContext.role != 'ADMIN'")
    public Product findById(Long id, RequestContext requestContext) {
        return productRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    }

    // Stampede protection — only one thread refreshes on miss
    @Cacheable(value = "pricing", key = "#sku", sync = true)
    public BigDecimal getPrice(String sku) {
        return pricingEngine.calculatePrice(sku);
    }
}
```

**`condition`** prevents cache lookup entirely. **`unless`** executes the method but skips storing the result. **`sync = true`** synchronizes cache population per key, preventing thundering-herd stampedes.

Create a configuration class to customize session behavior:

```java
@Configuration
@EnableRedisHttpSession(
    maxInactiveIntervalInSeconds = 1800, // 30 minutes
    redisNamespace = "myapp:session",     // Key prefix in Redis
    flushMode = FlushMode.ON_SAVE         // Batch writes until response commit
)
public class SessionConfig {

    @Bean
    public CookieSerializer cookieSerializer() {
        DefaultCookieSerializer serializer = new DefaultCookieSerializer();
        serializer.setCookieName("SESSIONID");
        serializer.setDomainNamePattern("^.+?(\\.[\\w]+\\.[\\w]+)$");
        serializer.setSameSite("Lax");
        serializer.setUseSecureCookie(true);
        return serializer;
    }

    // For REST APIs: propagate session ID via X-Auth-Token header
    @Bean
    public HttpSessionIdResolver httpSessionIdResolver() {
        return HeaderHttpSessionIdResolver.xAuthToken();
    }
}
```

The `redisNamespace` prevents key collisions between environments or applications sharing the same Redis instance.

Use `HttpSession` exactly as before — Spring Session transparently redirects to Redis:

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class CartController {

    @PostMapping("/cart/add")
    public ResponseEntity<Cart> addToCart(
            HttpSession session,
            @RequestBody CartItem item) {
        // getAttribute/setAttribute work exactly like in-memory sessions
        Cart cart = (Cart) session.getAttribute("cart");
        if (cart == null) {
            cart = new Cart();
        }
        cart.addItem(item);
        session.setAttribute("cart", cart); // Persisted to Redis
        return ResponseEntity.ok(cart);
    }

    @GetMapping("/cart")
    public ResponseEntity<Cart> getCart(HttpSession session) {
        Cart cart = (Cart) session.getAttribute("cart");
        return ResponseEntity.ok(
            cart != null ? cart : new Cart());
    }

    @PostMapping("/logout")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> logout(HttpSession session) {
        session.invalidate(); // Removes from Redis immediately
        return ResponseEntity.noContent().build();
    }
}
```

**Important:** Every object stored via `setAttribute` must implement `Serializable`. For cleaner Redis storage, configure a JSON serializer on the `RedisTemplate` used by Spring Session. Keep session payloads small — every request reads and writes the full session hash.
